import{_ as s,o as t,c as o,ag as n}from"./chunks/framework.DQQHyi2x.js";const u=JSON.parse('{"title":"Harnesses","description":"","frontmatter":{},"headers":[],"relativePath":"features/harnesses.md","filePath":"features/harnesses.md"}'),a={name:"features/harnesses.md"};function i(r,e,d,l,c,p){return t(),o("div",null,[...e[0]||(e[0]=[n('<h1 id="harnesses" tabindex="-1">Harnesses <a class="header-anchor" href="#harnesses" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Harnesses&quot;">​</a></h1><p>Harnesses provides operation details, configuration and session entry points even when no repositories are watched. All six harness cards use the existing provider registry plus the versioned operations/readiness catalog. Factory delivery filters and the saved native-provider preference remain independent.</p><p>Binary status, reported integration capabilities, configured harness and observed bound session are different observations. An installed binary does not prove credential readiness or that an agent is bound to a team. A positive capability flag does not create a page or authorize a write.</p><p>Claude has the implemented native surfaces recorded in the registry. Codex has a configuration summary, configuration, MCP, plugins and read-only Plans adapters, with separate nested action guards. Copilot, OpenCode, Pi and Muse Code have no dedicated settings adapters in this registry; their CLI/integration capabilities and shared Teams/Bridge operations remain separate. Page availability intersects the required catalog&#39;s availability, exact adapter identity and access with the checked-in adapter and both native capability classifications. Missing, malformed, failed, expired or mismatched catalog data grants no native access. Canonical routes, compatibility aliases, detail routes, dashboard destinations and native API calls use this restriction. A writable legacy editor without a read-only presentation does not mount when access is narrowed to read-only; nested MCP/plugin mutations remain separately guarded.</p><p>Choose a native page for its explicit harness without changing the saved global preference. Use Teams and launch planning for a manual agent, review the current selected-slot plan, and invoke launch as a separate authenticated action. Bridge provides live-session inspection. Selecting Harnesses or navigating to a plan does not start automatic dispatch, launch an agent or grant agent approval.</p><p>Each card classifies ten operations as supported, conditional, unsupported or unknown and lists the conditions. Native feature counts do not determine operating support. Conditional support still requires current identity, workspace and approval authority; navigating this index does not supply any of them. Background revalidation preserves unsaved native editor input while the actual last validated catalog is still within its 60-second lifetime. Catalog requests have a 10-second deadline; timed-out flights are aborted and retired so later completions cannot restore permissions or prevent recovery. Failed reads and expiry revoke access, including on visible-tab resume during a stalled refresh; static permissions never replace missing catalog evidence.</p><p>Configuration <strong>ready</strong> is shown as <strong>Configured for launch</strong>, using the binary/Mail prerequisites shared with launch planning. Configuration blocked and unknown results include the available reasons. Negative legacy Mail flags cannot distinguish confirmed absence from failed probes, so their check remains unknown even when lack of positive configuration evidence blocks launch. Credential readiness remains <strong>unknown</strong> because no account/model check is performed. Generic provider cards have <strong>unknown</strong> session readiness; only a separately scoped API read with verified team/slot/member/session/pane evidence can establish a binding.</p><p>Local observation is shared across cards by one snapshot worker per API process, cached for 60 seconds. Requests wait at most two seconds and can show pending checks. A 90-second observation deadline initiates cleanup of the trusted observer-owned process group, with descendant reaping awaited before completion or refresh. The disposable Linux cleanup supervisor does not enable subreaping in the API process. Unavailable cleanup support or failed probes yields unknown readiness; this is not hostile-process containment or a precise OS-reap wallclock guarantee. Existing local version/runtime/MCP-inventory checks remain non-billable; no model calls, credential refreshes or disposable production sessions are used. Observations do not establish credential access, enforced execution isolation, milestone acceptance, pilot disposition or delivery reliability.</p><p>OpenCode requires version 2 or later. With OpenCode 1 or an unreadable version, the OpenCode card shows a blocked configuration with the reason &quot;OpenCode 2 or later is required&quot;. Launch planning and the Live sessions dialog refuse to start OpenCode with the block code <code>opencode_version_unsupported</code>. Albiex Synth City also does not adopt, register in Agent Mail or wake a running OpenCode member unless its process runs the OpenCode 2 binary that Synth City found on its own <code>PATH</code>. A member started before OpenCode was updated in place must be relaunched.</p><p>OpenCode members launch as <code>opencode &lt;directory&gt; --standalone --session &lt;id&gt; [--auto] [--prompt &lt;text&gt;]</code>. <code>--standalone</code> gives each member its own private server, so its tools and Agent Mail run in its own process tree. The shared background service (<code>opencode serve --service</code>) is never treated as a member.</p><ul><li><strong>Model and agent:</strong> the OpenCode 2 TUI has no <code>--model</code> or <code>--agent</code> flag. Synth City passes the slot&#39;s model (<code>provider/model</code>, with an optional <code>#variant</code>) and default agent in the member&#39;s <code>OPENCODE_CONFIG_CONTENT</code>. It also turns off updates and allows only the model&#39;s provider; with no model it disables OpenCode&#39;s hosted free models. The config holds no secret, because the member&#39;s plugins can read it.</li><li><strong>Exact session:</strong> on a slot&#39;s first launch, Synth City chooses the session id (<code>ses_</code> and 26 letters and digits), saves it on the slot and resumes with <code>--session &lt;saved id&gt;</code>. <code>--continue</code> is not offered; a slot still stored with &quot;use last&quot; is blocked with <code>opencode_continue_unsupported</code>.</li><li><strong>Permissions:</strong> the slot&#39;s skip-permissions option maps to <code>--auto</code>, which approves prompts that no rule denies. Deny rules still apply.</li><li><strong>Bedrock:</strong> use an <code>amazon-bedrock/…</code> model.</li><li><strong>Questions:</strong> each member&#39;s launch config and the Agent Mail install deny OpenCode&#39;s question tool; an open question or permission prompt is escalated to the Supervisor as waiting for input (see <a href="/docs/features/agent-mail.html">Agent Mail</a>).</li><li><strong>Ownership:</strong> an OpenCode member can own GitHub work items. Activation readiness and the owner-observation pause accept it only when its process is the exact <code>--standalone --session &lt;saved id&gt;</code> member on OpenCode 2, and its Agent Mail registration comes from that pane or its own private server.</li><li><strong>Safety guard:</strong> the team supervisor checks an OpenCode member&#39;s own replies for provider safety notices, as it does for Claude Code, Codex and Muse Code. OpenCode 2 keeps replies in SQLite, so on every check the guard reads, read-only, all assistant text of the bound session (<code>ses_</code> id and workspace directory) from <code>opencode.db</code> under the configured OpenCode data directories. Reasoning, tool output and the user&#39;s prompts never cause a hold. It refuses (and stops an armed team) when the database is missing, ambiguous, replaced, not a plain file owned by the agent user, or not in the expected table shape, and when a size, row, time or work limit is exceeded. It never skips text it can&#39;t read. The table check validates the required columns only; it doesn&#39;t prove which OpenCode version wrote them. The guard records the database file&#39;s identity on first sight, and checks that the file SQLite actually holds open is that file, using the supervisor&#39;s own <code>/proc/self/fd</code> (Linux only; if it can&#39;t read that evidence, it refuses). This catches a replaced or swapped-in file, but not a file rewritten in place, including its write-ahead log: an agent that owns its data directory can still rewrite its own history. A replaced database stays refused until an operator clears that session&#39;s recorded file identity in the guard state.</li><li><strong>Not yet available:</strong> Synth City doesn&#39;t show an OpenCode member as working or idle; its activity stays unknown, apart from waiting for input (see Questions) and whether its process is running. The working and idle states, the question and idle screen fallback, and a live check with a real OpenCode member are still open.</li></ul><p>Codex has no native Backup/Usage/Context/transcript adapter here; export and diagnostics API support is separate from browser-page access. See restore/usage limits in <a href="/docs/guide/multi-provider-codex-v2.html">Multi-Provider and Codex CLI</a>. See <a href="/docs/features/agent-teams.html">Teams</a> for reviewed launch/reuse and <a href="/docs/features/agent-bridge.html">Live sessions</a> for standalone work. Existing Pi opt-in/runtime/exact-resume requirements remain; this index changes neither integration nor sandboxing.</p><h2 id="muse-code" tabindex="-1">Muse Code <a class="header-anchor" href="#muse-code" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Muse Code&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Muse Code is Meta&#39;s CLI (<code>muse</code>). Albiex Synth City runs it as a team member with the provider <code>muse-cli</code>, for example with the model <code>muse-wonder-1.0</code>.</p><ul><li><strong>Subscription:</strong> a Muse subscription works only inside Muse Code. Synth City therefore launches Muse Code itself; it does not run the Muse models in another harness.</li><li><strong>Launch:</strong> <code>muse --trust-workspace --model &lt;model&gt; [--reasoning-effort &lt;tier&gt;] [--yolo] [&lt;prompt&gt;]</code>. <strong>Skip permissions</strong> maps to <code>--yolo</code>. Muse Code cannot be given a session id, so Synth City discovers it from the <code>SessionStart</code> hook or from the session lock that the process holds, saves it on the slot, and relaunches with <code>muse resume &lt;session id&gt;</code>.</li><li><strong>Agent Mail:</strong> <strong>Set up</strong> on the Muse Code page writes the user-wide settings file <code>~/.config/muse/settings.json</code> (or <code>$MUSE_CONFIG_DIR/settings.json</code>). It adds the <code>albiex-synth-city-mail</code> MCP server and the <code>SessionStart</code> and <code>UserPromptSubmit</code> hooks, and keeps every other setting. <strong>Remove</strong> deletes only Synth City’s entries. Synth City refuses to change a file that it cannot read as settings.</li><li><strong>Questions:</strong> a hook cannot block Muse Code&#39;s question tool. Synth City reads the session&#39;s event log, shows a question as <strong>waiting for input</strong>, and notifies the bound Supervisor. Team charters should tell Muse members to write to the Supervisor instead of asking.</li><li><strong>Updates:</strong> the <code>muse</code> launcher updates Muse Code by itself. Each observation records the session&#39;s Muse Code version and model, so a change during a campaign is visible. Synth City does not pin the version.</li></ul>',15)])])}const g=s(a,[["render",i]]);export{u as __pageData,g as default};
